ip-z80.asm, slip-z80.asm, tcp-z80.asm -- the stack written by hand
==================================================================

Three drop-in replacements for the objects sccz80 produces from `ip.c`,
`slip.c` and `tcp.c`.  Same module names (`ip_c`, `slip_c`, `tcp_c`), same
entry points, same __smallc calling conventions, so the linker cannot tell
them apart:

        make                    # links ip.o, slip.o, tcp.o
        make Z80_ASM=1          # links ip-z80.o, slip-z80.o, tcp-z80.o

One layer at a time, for bisecting a problem on real hardware, with
`Z80_ASM_IP`, `Z80_ASM_SLIP` and `Z80_ASM_TCP`.  Each defaults to
`Z80_ASM`, and anything other than empty or 0 turns it on:

        make Z80_ASM_TCP=1              # only the TCP layer
        make Z80_ASM=1 Z80_ASM_TCP=0    # everything except the TCP layer

The `.c` files stay in the tree and remain the specification.  Read those
to find out what the stack does; read these to find out how it is encoded.
`icmp.c` is left alone -- it is only linked into `ping`.

What it costs and what it buys
------------------------------

                        C       asm      delta
        ip.o          463       240       -223
        slip.o        419       277       -142   (+9 bytes bss)
        tcp.o        2004      1163       -841   (+14 bytes bss)
                     ----      ----      -----
                     2886      1680      -1206

        ping.bin     8352      6837      -1515
        tcp_chat.bin 10473     9004      -1469
        irc.bin      15610     14141     -1469
        tiny-irc.bin  8580      7106      -1474

The binaries drop more than the objects do, because the C versions also
drag in library helpers that nothing else uses -- htonl,
l_glong/l_plong/l_inclong, l_long_add/com/eq/ne/ult/ugt, l_gt, l_lt,
l_lneg, memcmp_callee, and the whole l_gintNsp family.

Per layer, measured on `tiny-irc.bin`:

        Z80_ASM_IP=1     8344   -236
        Z80_ASM_SLIP=1   8429   -151
        Z80_ASM_TCP=1    7538  -1042
        Z80_ASM=1        7106  -1474

For `tiny-irc.bin` that last number is the interesting one.  Its budget is
24000..32767 = 8768 bytes for code, data and stack; it was using 8580 of
that, leaving 188 bytes of headroom.  It now uses 7106, leaving 1662.

Where the 1206 bytes went
-------------------------

sccz80 is not a bad compiler, it just has no register allocator worth the
name and pays for every access.  In `tcp.c`:

  * Struct fields go through `l_gint` / `l_pint` / `l_glong` / `l_plong`
    and arguments through `l_gintspsp` -- a `call` plus setup for every
    single field.  Here IX is the frame pointer and the struct is walked
    with HL.

  * `ip_buffer(&tcp->ip, n)` is a function call in `tcp.c`.  It is
    `ip->pkt + n`.

  * The 20 byte TCP header is built with a dozen two-byte `memcpy()`s,
    each of which is a `ld bc,2` plus `ldir`.  Here it is one walk down
    the struct: `saddr` and `daddr` are adjacent so one `LDIR` does both,
    the two ports are four `LDI`s, and `htonl()` of `seq` and `ack` is a
    four byte reversed copy instead of a library call through a 32 bit
    temporary.

  * The sequence number comparisons were `l_long_eq` / `l_long_ne` /
    `l_long_ult` / `l_long_ugt`, each with its operands marshalled onto
    the stack.  They are now one 24 byte `cmp32` and one 9 byte `inc32`.

  * `oseq` in `tcp_send()` is assigned and never read.  The compiler
    still emits the 32 bit load and store; this does not.

In `ip.c`:

  * `csum()` accumulates into a `uint32_t`, so every 16 bit word of the
    packet costs an `l_long_add` through a stack temporary.  `ip-z80.asm`
    folds the carry back into a 16 bit accumulator as it happens -- the
    end around carry of RFC 1071 -- and does no 32 bit arithmetic at all.
    That is the same one's complement sum: both are the total modulo
    65535, and the accumulator cannot overflow, because to hold 0xffff
    *and* produce a carry an addition would have to reach 0x1ffff and the
    largest possible is 0xffff + 0xffff = 0x1fffe.  219 bytes -> 64.

  * `ip_build_and_send()` writes the header with 2 and 4 byte
    `memcpy()`s and reads `ip->id`, `ip->saddr` and `ip->daddr` through
    the long helpers.  Here HL walks the packet from byte 0 to byte 19
    once, and `saddr` and `daddr` are adjacent in `ip_context_t` so one
    LDIR does both.  227 bytes -> 160.

In `slip.c`:

  * `rs232_put()` and `rs232_get()` are the Interface 1 ROM's BCHAN-OUT
    and BCHAN-IN, which destroy AF, BC, DE and HL and leave only IX and
    IY.  sccz80 handles that by spilling everything to the stack and
    reloading through `l_gintNsp` around every call.  `send_packet()`
    keeps its pointer in IX instead and only writes the counter back;
    `recv_packet()` keeps its state in file statics.

  * `recv_packet()` tracks `len - received` and `&p[received]` instead of
    `received` and `len`, so the "is there room" test is a test for zero
    and the store needs no indexing.  262 bytes -> 154, and
    `send_packet()` 115 -> 86.

Things to know before touching them
-----------------------------------

  * **IX does not survive a call into compiler generated code.**  sccz80's
    `l_long_add` returns with `pop ix / jp (ix)`, and `csum()` does 32 bit
    arithmetic, so `_csum` and `_ip_build_and_send` both come back with IX
    destroyed.  Every call to `_csum`, `_ip_build_and_send` and
    `_recv_packet` is bracketed with `push ix` / `pop ix`.  The six entry
    points in this file save and restore IX themselves, so they are safe
    to call from C and safe to call each other.

  * **`seq`, `ack`, `tmp` and `ptr` in `tcp_recv_timeout()` are file
    statics, not stack locals.**  That function is re-entrant --
    `tcp_recv_timeout -> tcp_send -> tcp_recv -> tcp_recv_timeout` -- so
    this is only correct because of an invariant in `tcp.c`: every use of
    those four is finished before any of the `tcp_send()` calls is made,
    and the `goto again` path reloads all of them.  `len` is *not* static
    for exactly that reason: it is read again after the last
    `tcp_send()`, so it stays in the frame at `ix+6`.  If `tcp.c` ever
    grows a use of `seq` or `ack` after a `tcp_send()`, this has to
    become stack locals.

  * `rt_ack` must stay immediately below `rt_seq` in the bss.  Reading the
    two 32 bit numbers out of the packet is one descending 8 byte copy
    that relies on it.

  * The retry counts are countdowns, so they are off by one from the
    `retries++ < 5` in `tcp.c`.  `tcp_send()` starts at 6 (one send plus
    five retransmissions); `tcp_connect()` starts at 5 (five attempts).

  * **`recv_packet()`'s state is static too**, for the same reason: the
    only thing it calls is `rs232_get()`.  `rp_c` has to be zeroed on
    entry the way `slip.c`'s `uint8_t c = 0` is -- a timed out
    `rs232_get()` does not write to it, so a leftover END from the
    previous packet would end the wait loop immediately.

  * `RS232_TPS` and `SLIP_ESC_00` are `defc` / `IFDEF`, defaulted the way
    the C files are and overridden from the Makefile with
    `-Ca-DRS232_TPS=n` and `-Ca-DSLIP_ESC_00`, mirroring the `-D` the C
    files get.  `SLIP_ESC_00` still has to match `slip-tun`.

Testing
-------

        make test-z80           # or test-ip / test-slip / test-tcp

Each of the three builds its test program twice -- once against the `.c`
file, once against the `.asm` -- runs both under `z88dk-ticks` and diffs
the output.  These are Z80 programs linked against the rest of the real
stack, not host recompiles, so it is the actual Spectrum code that runs.

`test/test_ip.c` mocks `send_packet()` and covers `csum()` over four
patterns at every length from 0 to 41 -- all zeros, all 0xff (a carry out
of every single addition, and a sum that has to stay 0xffff rather than
drop to zero), a carry on every other word, and a counting pattern -- plus
odd lengths, a real header, `ip_build_and_send()` with several lengths and
protocols, the `ip->id` increment across both byte boundaries, that the
payload after the header is left alone, and `ip_buffer()` including a
negative offset.

`test/test_slip.c` uses `test/stub232.asm`, which provides the four rs232
entry points with the real calling conventions and forwards them to C
mocks.  It covers `slip_init()` and both of its failure paths, the
arguments it passes on, `send_packet()` with every escape and with a zero
length, and `recv_packet()`: leading junk, escapes, empty frames, buffer
overrun, an unknown escape, no END at all, an exact and a one-short
timeout, and `to` = 1.  It counts `rs232_get()` calls as well as comparing
the bytes, so the timeout arithmetic is pinned down too.

There is deliberately no `len == 0` case for `recv_packet()`: `slip.c`
never returns from one, because `received` can then never become non-zero,
so there is nothing there to differ on.

`test/test_tcp.c` mocks only `slip.c` -- `send_packet()` hexdumps the
packet, `recv_packet()` replays a scripted one -- and covers the SYN
handshake and its failure paths, receiving data, larger TCP headers, zero
window probes, retransmissions, RST, the four packet sanity checks, short
packets, opening and clamping the window, sending data from an argument
and from the tx buffer, the retransmit-and-give-up path, bare ACKs, and
both buffer accessors.  Every sent packet is compared byte for byte,
including the checksum, and so is the resulting connection state.

The one thing it does not pin down is the source port after a failed
`tcp_connect()`, because that is seeded from the Z80 `R` register and the
two versions do not execute the same number of instructions.  Those
scenarios compare the return code and the number of packets instead.

These need z88dk on the PATH; `make test` deliberately does not, which is
why they are separate targets.  Note `z88dk-ticks` segfaults if the binary
it is given has no dot in its filename -- it calls `strcasecmp()` on the
result of `strchr(name, '.')` -- hence the `.bin` suffixes.
